{
 "_comment": [
  "AI TECH-TREE registry - the single source of the WA_AI_TECHTREE_* triggers in",
  "common/scripted_triggers/WA_AI_TECHTREE_gates.txt (GENERATED by tools/gen/gen_techtree_gates.py).",
  "One entry per CAPABILITY. Under `trees`, one entry per TECHNOLOGY FOLDER (the folder ids of",
  "common/technology_tags/00_technology.txt): the listed rungs are the ones that open the capability",
  "FOR A COUNTRY THAT HAS THAT FOLDER. Membership is asked by WA_AI_TECHTREE_has_<folder> in",
  "WA_AI_TECHTREE_membership.txt (GENERATED from the same folders), so a country handed another",
  "tree by event counts as a member of it - which is the point of the layer.",
  "A folder ABSENT from a capability is barred from it: that tree simply has no such branch, and",
  "`has_branch` says so. That is a DECISION, never an oversight - the generator --check reports a",
  "folder present in a sibling capability but absent here.",
  "`granted` lists rungs that live in NO folder (allow = { always = no }, reachable only through",
  "set_technology from a focus or an event). They carry no membership term: whoever was granted the",
  "tech has it, whatever tree they own.",
  "`branch_extra` names folders that DO carry the branch but whose rungs are deliberately not in `trees`: has_branch counts them, availability does not. It exists because the two answers have different failure modes - a folder missing from has_branch stops a country weighting research for a technology it can actually reach, while adding its rungs to `trees` would hand it a template. Every entry carries its reason. The generator --check re-derives the candidate list from common/technologies/ and WARNs on a folder that is in neither."
 ],
